[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Architecture, Master-Agent Topologies, and Executor Allocation in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jenkins utilizes a distributed client-server architecture designed to scale continuous integration and continuous delivery workloads. Understanding how the Jenkins master coordinates with agents and allocates executors is vital for optimizing build performance, resource utilization, and pipeline security."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Jenkins Master Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Jenkins master is the central control unit of your CI/CD infrastructure. Its primary responsibilities include scheduling jobs, dispatching builds to agents, monitoring agent health, recording and presenting build results, and serving the web user interface."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Job Scheduling: Evaluates trigger conditions, manages the build queue, and determines execution order."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Plugin Management: Hosts the core engine and manages lifecycle events for installed extensions."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configuration Store: Maintains job definitions, pipeline scripts, system settings, and credential stores."},{"order":8,"type":"heading","text":"Master-Agent Topologies"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Running all builds on the master node is discouraged in production environments due to security risks and resource contention. Instead, Jenkins employs a master-agent topology where the master delegates actual execution workloads to remote or local worker nodes known as agents."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static Agents: Dedicated virtual machines, physical servers, or containers permanently attached to the master."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic Agents: Ephemeral nodes spun up on-demand via cloud plugins (e.g., Kubernetes, Docker, AWS EC2) and terminated post-build."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Communication Protocols"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Agents communicate with the master over TCP using the Jenkins remoting library. Communication can be established via inbound connections (agent initiates connection to master using JNLP) or outbound connections (master initiates SSH connections to the agent)."},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Executor Allocation Mechanics"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"An executor is a slot for execution on a node. Each node can be configured with one or more executors depending on available CPU and memory resources. When a pipeline is triggered, the master evaluates node labels, workspace requirements, and executor availability to map the job to an optimal slot."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Node Labels and Agent Targeting"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Declarative pipelines allow precise control over executor allocation using the 'agent' block. By specifying node labels, you ensure workloads run on hardware matching specific requirements, such as operating system architecture or installed toolchains."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent {
        node {
            label 'linux-docker-agent'
        }
    }
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                sh 'mvn clean package'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices for Scalability"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never run heavy build workloads or tests directly on the Jenkins master node."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage containerized agents (like the Kubernetes plugin) to provide isolated, clean environments for every build."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tune the number of executors per agent based on IO and CPU constraints rather than core counts alone."}]
